  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Space Exploration Technologies shows $1.9B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Space Exploration Technologies received 63 awards so far and the average award size is $29.6M. Space Exploration Technologies obligations are down 37.9% year over year. Space Exploration Technologies has a multi-year profile that peaked at $3.4B in FY24 and then moved to $3B in FY25 before the current-year total of $1.9B. Space Exploration Technologies sales are concentrated in a few places: the Department of Defense obligated $1.9B in FY2026 to date and Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation accounts for $1.5B of activity in the same period. Space Exploration Technologies also has measurable activity in Satellite Telecommunications and R&D, with $280M and $100M respectively. Space Exploration Technologies has small or zero-dollar entries in several NAICS lines and minor obligations from non-DoD agencies such as NASA and the Department of State.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main federal buyers for Space Exploration Technologies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the main buyer and obligated $1.9B in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $6.4M and the Department of State obligated $3.1M. Other agencies reported negligible obligations, including the Department of Transportation at $9K and the Department of the Interior at $219."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and contract sizes drive Space Exploration Technologies revenue?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the largest market at $1.5B in FY2026 to date. Satellite Telecommunications and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences account for $280M and $100M respectively. The company has an average award size of $29.6M across 63 awards, and obligations are down 37.9% year over year."
      }
    ]
  },
